there are times when IM setsuna's  mimicry is too obvious for its own good  when you immediately recognize that it's  a deliberate effort to trigger nostalgia  for classic Japanese RPGs but these  moments don't tell the whole story  poignant events lend distinction to the  otherwise archetypal cast and you grew  to appreciate how tenets of the genre  are ignored in favor of trying something  new the juxtaposition of item sets in  his numerous inventions and references  make for an eye-opening examination of  Japanese RPGs and a satisfactory debut  from developer Tokyo RPG Factory  in a world blanketed with snow and  overridden with beasts young setsuna  sets out to reach the last lands it is  there that she will fulfill her duty as  a human sacrifice a ritual held every  decade to keep wild threats at bay with  the help of like-minded adventurers you  accompany setsuna through caverns and  forests fending off creatures and  fording or recruiting humans that try to  stand in your way  can easy  setsuna would be the first person to  admit that her fate is tragic but she  remains steadfast and resolute in her  charge you can say the same for her  companions some of whom face very  different but equally somber realities  with grace clocking in around 20 hours  moderately short by RPG standards it's  unfortunate that I am setsuna doesn't  allow more time for backstory and  character development the majority of  your venture is spent in combat which is  almost identical to chrono triggers  battle system and thankfully every bit  is enjoyable  there are no random encounters and I am  setsuna battles begin when you bump into  enemies visible in dungeons  there are no enemies on the world map  and there are two ways to approach  combat active mode or wait mode active  mode allows ATB gauges to fill on a  constant basis when that gauge is full  that ally or enemy can make a move if  you choose to play in weight mode all  ETB gauges stop charging when you're  picking an action from the command menu  affording you more time to plan your  next move every character has an  inherent strength weapon type and  selection of skills known as Tex techs  are a product of things called sprite  night magical stones that are slotted  into equipment certain characters can  combine text in combat to dish out a  devastating attack or a powered up  recovery spell so long as they both have  full atv gauges with a limited number of  slots for sprite night you have to  carefully pick and choose which text to  carry not only to have a well-rounded  team but to ensure the availability of  prefered combo attacks which are  strictly tied to specific text every  tech can be enhanced on-the-fly during  combat when a character builds up enough  SP to trigger momentum which requires  you to press a button immediately after  a flash of light during the character's  wind-up animation you earn SP when you  attack and you take damage and if you  wait when it's your turn to move  managing momentum opportunities is the  most critical element during fights as  it can in effect turn one action into to  exhume in typical RPG fashion characters  are an experience from combat and level  up but rather than impact every stat new  levels only boost a character's hit  points and magic points instead weapons  dictated characters physical and magical  strength as well as both types of  defense  therefore purchasing weapons becomes a  habit the moment you discover a new town  but you can't buy anything in IM setsuna  until you sell materials objects earned  in battle and found on the ground when  exploring dungeons because currency  isn't dulled out after fights or found  in treasure chests the reliance on  materials for currency and skills feels  different from most other RPGs as  perhaps the most resounding invention in  the game designed to be almost wholly  referential  by the end of volume sets enough mobs of  common enemies albeit strong ones can't  stand up to a well-oiled party and can  be wiped out with a single combo move  and a little added momentum as such it  can feel like you're going through the  motions at times when playing I am  setsuna this paired with obvious  references to well-known games can make  the experience feel rote and at worst  boring so I am setsuna is an  unapologetic homage to beloved Japanese  RPGs that plays well and takes few risks  Tokyo RPG Factory has accomplished their  implied mission statement to make  Japanese RPGs the way many of us  remember them back in the day as someone  who grew up playing these games I  enjoyed my time with i''m sets enough it  was nice to look back and remember the  good times
 

